Chapter: DeskWindowFrameTest
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopFont
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The first window's frame: where it sits, what the pointer lands on, what
 maximising moves, and what PLACING it moves.

 `dk-wnd-hit` is the whole of the second question and it is a pure function
 of a rectangle, the point, the face and the width scale, so it is asserted
 directly rather than through a paint. The face is `gfont-none` here, which is
 the CBF fallback branch of `dk-wnd-bar-h`: a titlebar height that does not
 depend on a volume being mounted is what lets this arm carry a real
 expectation instead of the no-volume line the other desk arms record.

 The `ds` block is 128 bytes because that is what `desk-run` allocates. A
 64-byte block cannot legally hold the cells above 63 -- `dk-icons-cell` at 72
 and `dk-task-band-cell` at 76 -- and a short block reads whatever the arena
 put after it rather than the zero the fallbacks are written for.

 BOTH WIDTHS AGAIN. The window rectangle derives from the content box, which
 carries the 1024 and 1600 scale steps, and a frame that is correct at one
 width says nothing about the other (`desk-pane-origin` records why).

 BOTH STATES, for the same reason one level up. Every row below runs normal
 and maximised, because a rect that is right in one state says nothing about
 the other and the buttons move with it: `dk-wnd-btn-x` is measured from the
 window's right edge, which is the whole content box when maximised.

 The button row is ordered min, max, close from the left, and the assertion
 that matters is that the three are DISTINCT and in that order: a strip that
 answers the same code for all three, or answers them right-to-left, paints
 identically and closes the window when you meant to minimise it.

 EVERY ROW READS THE RECT THROUGH `dk-wnd-rx` AND ITS THREE SIBLINGS over a
 block nothing has placed, so this file's expectation not moving is the
 calibration: the never-placed fallback answers exactly what the arithmetic
 answered before a rectangle could be stored at all. The placed rows at the
 end are the half that could not be written before.

Section: Body

  wf-yn : Boolean -> Text
  wf-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

  wf-mid : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wf-mid (wx) (ww) (ws) (i) =
    dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww gfont-none ws i + dk-wnd-bar-h gfont-none ws / 2

  wf-name : Integer -> Text
  wf-name (st) = if st == dk-wnd-st-max then "max   " else "normal"

  wf-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wf-row (w) (h) (st) (ds) (blk) =
    let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let side = dk-wnd-bar-h gfont-none ws
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in let wh = dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds
    in let barmid = wy + side / 2
    in "w " & integer-to-text w & " " & wf-name st
       & "  x " & integer-to-text wx & " y " & integer-to-text wy
       & " w " & integer-to-text ww & " h " & integer-to-text wh
       & "  bar " & integer-to-text side
       & "  min " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-hit wx wy ww wh (wf-mid wx ww ws 0) barmid gfont-none ws)
       & " max " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-hit wx wy ww wh (wf-mid wx ww ws 1) barmid gfont-none ws)
       & " close " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-hit wx wy ww wh (wf-mid wx ww ws 2) barmid gfont-none ws)
       & " bar " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-hit wx wy ww wh (wx + 8) barmid gfont-none ws)
       & " body " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-hit wx wy ww wh (wx + 8) (wy + side + 8) gfont-none ws)
       & " out " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-hit wx wy ww wh (wx - 8) barmid gfont-none ws)

 The frame must sit inside what the chrome leaves, the way the panes did not
 before WORKS-35. A maximised window is the content box exactly, so this is
 the row that would catch a maximise that filled the SCREEN instead. The left
 comparison is against `dk-cbox-x`, which is what every pane and every window
 inherits; it answers zero now that the sidebar is gone, and it is still the
 one function that would move if a gutter came back.

 The taskbar edge is `dk-task-px`, not the floor constant, and that matters
 now that the band can be taller than its floor: comparing the window against
 the constant would agree whatever the band actually did.

  wf-inside : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wf-inside (w) (h) (st) (ds) (blk) =
    let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let wx = dk-wnd-rx blk w st
    in let wy = dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds
    in let ww = dk-wnd-rw blk w st
    in "w " & integer-to-text w & " " & wf-name st
       & "  inside the content box " & wf-yn (wx >= dk-cbox-x w)
       & "  below the top bar " & wf-yn (wy >= dk-bar-h * ui-scale w)
       & "  above the taskbar " & wf-yn (wy + dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds <= h - dk-task-px ds w)
       & "  buttons distinct and in order " & wf-yn (dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww gfont-none ws 0 < dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww gfont-none ws 1 & dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww gfont-none ws 1 < dk-wnd-btn-x wx ww gfont-none ws 2)

 THE ROW THAT DISCRIMINATES, and the reason it uses this point and not the
 window's own corner. Every row above reads the rect and then asks about a
 point derived FROM that rect, so it moves with the window and would answer
 the same thing if maximising did nothing at all. This one fixes one point in
 SCREEN coordinates -- just inside the content box's top-left, on the titlebar
 row a maximised window would have there -- and asks what lands on it in each
 state. Maximised the window starts at that corner, so it answers `bar`;
 normal the window is inset by an eighth of the box and the same point is
 outside it, so it answers `none`. Two different answers from one point is
 what says the rect actually moved.

  wf-corner : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wf-corner (w) (h) (st) (ds) (blk) =
    let ws = ui-wscale w
    in let side = dk-wnd-bar-h gfont-none ws
    in dk-wnd-hit (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) (dk-cbox-x w + 8) (dk-cbox-y w + side / 2) gfont-none ws

  wf-max : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wf-max (w) (h) (ds) (blk) =
    "w " & integer-to-text w
    & "  maximised is the content box "
    & wf-yn (dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-max == dk-cbox-x w
             & dk-wnd-ry blk w h dk-wnd-st-max ds == dk-cbox-y w
             & dk-wnd-rw blk w dk-wnd-st-max == dk-cbox-w w
             & dk-wnd-rh blk w h dk-wnd-st-max ds == dk-cbox-h w h ds)
    & "  the two rects differ "
    & wf-yn (dk-wnd-rw blk w dk-wnd-st-max /= dk-wnd-rw blk w dk-wnd-st-normal)
    & "  one screen point: max " & integer-to-text (wf-corner w h dk-wnd-st-max ds blk)
    & " normal " & integer-to-text (wf-corner w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds blk)

 A never-placed block must answer exactly the arithmetic, or every picture the
 desk has drawn since the first window moved. This is the calibration row and
 it is the one that would go red if the sentinel were read the other way
 round.

  wf-default : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wf-default (w) (h) (st) (ds) (blk) =
    "w " & integer-to-text w & " " & wf-name st
    & "  placed " & wf-yn (dk-wnd-placed blk)
    & "  matches the arithmetic "
    & wf-yn (dk-wnd-rx blk w st == dk-wnd-x w st
             & dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds == dk-wnd-y w h st ds
             & dk-wnd-rw blk w st == dk-wnd-w w st
             & dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds == dk-wnd-h w h st ds)

 THE HALF THAT COULD NOT BE WRITTEN BEFORE. A rectangle nobody chose is the
 same rectangle for every window, so two windows landed on the same pixels and
 no hit test could tell them apart. These rows place one somewhere the default
 is not and ask the same questions of it.

 The discriminating point is the CENTRE of the default rect, in screen
 coordinates, and it is chosen for the reason `wf-corner` is: it does not
 travel with the window under test. The default window contains its own
 centre, so an unplaced block answers `body` there; the placed rect is a small
 box in the content box's top-left corner and does not reach it, so it answers
 `none`.

 THE TWO ROWS CATCH DIFFERENT THINGS AND THE SABOTAGES SAY WHICH, because the
 obvious reading -- that the centre point catches any placement that is read
 and ignored -- is wrong and was written here before it was measured. Three
 sabotages, 2026-08-25, against this expectation:

 | what was made to ignore the block | `reads back` | the centre point |
 |---|---|---|
 | `dk-wnd-rx` alone | yes to no | unmoved at 0 |
 | `dk-wnd-rw` alone | yes to no | unmoved at 0 |
 | `dk-wnd-placed`, so all four | yes to no | 0 to 5 |

 The centre point cannot move while any ONE of the four still reads the block,
 because the placed rect is small in both axes and the remaining cell keeps
 the point outside it. So `reads back` is the row with per-cell sensitivity
 and the centre point is the row that says placement is read AT ALL. Neither
 is redundant and neither covers the other. The maximised control and all ten
 rows above were unmoved by every one of the three.

  wf-place-x : Integer -> Integer
  wf-place-x (w) = dk-cbox-x w + 40

  wf-place-y : Integer -> Integer
  wf-place-y (w) = dk-cbox-y w + 30

  wf-mid-x : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wf-mid-x (w) (st) = dk-wnd-x w st + dk-wnd-w w st / 2

  wf-mid-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wf-mid-y (w) (h) (st) (ds) = dk-wnd-y w h st ds + dk-wnd-h w h st ds / 2

  wf-centre : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wf-centre (w) (h) (st) (ds) (blk) =
    dk-wnd-hit (dk-wnd-rx blk w st) (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds) (dk-wnd-rw blk w st) (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds) (wf-mid-x w dk-wnd-st-normal) (wf-mid-y w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds) gfont-none (ui-wscale w)

  wf-placed-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wf-placed-row (w) (h) (ds) (blk) =
    "w " & integer-to-text w
    & "  placed " & wf-yn (dk-wnd-placed blk)
    & "  reads back "
    & wf-yn (dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-normal == wf-place-x w
             & dk-wnd-ry blk w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds == wf-place-y w
             & dk-wnd-rw blk w dk-wnd-st-normal == 300
             & dk-wnd-rh blk w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds == 200)
    & "  moved off the default "
    & wf-yn (dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-normal /= dk-wnd-x w dk-wnd-st-normal)
    & "  the default centre: placed " & integer-to-text (wf-centre w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds blk)

 THE ROW THE CHANGE CANNOT MOVE, and it is here on purpose. Maximised is the
 content box whatever the block holds, so placement must not reach it. If it
 ever does, this row is what says so while every row above still agrees.

  wf-max-control : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wf-max-control (w) (h) (ds) (blk) =
    "w " & integer-to-text w
    & "  a placed window still maximises to the content box "
    & wf-yn (dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-max == dk-cbox-x w
             & dk-wnd-ry blk w h dk-wnd-st-max ds == dk-cbox-y w
             & dk-wnd-rw blk w dk-wnd-st-max == dk-cbox-w w
             & dk-wnd-rh blk w h dk-wnd-st-max ds == dk-cbox-h w h ds)

Section: Entry

 The two blocks are bound ONCE here rather than at module level. A nullary
 definition is a recipe and not a cell, so every mention of one would allocate
 a fresh zeroed block and the placement below would be written to a block
 nothing else ever reads.

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let fresh = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let held = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in act
      print-line-uni (wf-row 1280 800 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-row 1280 800 dk-wnd-st-max ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-row 1600 900 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-row 1600 900 dk-wnd-st-max ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-inside 1280 800 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-inside 1280 800 dk-wnd-st-max ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-inside 1600 900 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-inside 1600 900 dk-wnd-st-max ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-max 1280 800 ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-max 1600 900 ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-default 1280 800 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh)
      print-line-uni (wf-default 1600 900 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh)
      print-line-uni ("unplaced, the default centre: " & integer-to-text (wf-centre 1600 900 dk-wnd-st-normal ds fresh))
      let p = dk-wnd-place held (wf-place-x 1600) (wf-place-y 1600) 300 200
      in act
        print-line-uni (wf-placed-row 1600 900 ds held)
        print-line-uni (wf-max-control 1600 900 ds held)
        print-line-uni ("codes: min " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-min & " max " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-max & " close " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-close & " bar " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-bar & " body " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-body & " none " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-none)
        print-line-uni ("states: normal " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-st-normal & " max " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-st-max)
      end
    end

